// llmsPage is the page `fitness llms` prints and llms.txt holds.
const llmsPage = "# fitness\n\nfitness runs a repo's fitness checks: small programs that each judge one rule, such as spelling, tests, or pull request shape. One binary holds every built-in check; a repo may add local checks. Each check decides whether it applies, so any repo runs the same suite with nothing to configure.\n\nA run resolves the checks, runs them side by side under two totals, and prints a table of passed, failed, or info results. Info never fails a run. With files staged it judges only those files; --all judges every tracked file.\n\n## Exit codes\n\n- 0: every check passed, or a command succeeded.\n- 1: a check found problems, or the run could not start, such as a bad config.\n- 2: a command got arguments it does not take, or explain or docs named an unknown check or topic.\n\n## Learn the rest\n\n`fitness list` names every check, `fitness explain <check>` says why one exists and how to pass it, and `fitness docs` serves the guides. All of them, and this page, come from the code's own doc comments.\n\n## Run checks\n\n- `fitness` or `fitness <check> [args]`: Runs the full suite, or with a check name only that check, passing it the arguments after its name.\n- `fitness --all`: Forces a full scan even when files are staged.\n- `fitness --blob <text>`: Runs every blob-capable check on a text instead of the repo; - reads the text from stdin.\n- `fitness --dir=<folder>`: Judges only the files under one folder.\n- `fitness --file=<path>`: Judges only the named file; repeat it to name more.\n- `fitness --json=<file>`: Names the file that receives the run's performance report: each check's time, stage, files, binary size, and error lines, as JSON.\n- `fitness --no-builtins [args]`: Turns the built-in checks off, so a run uses only installed checks, as a clean install would; it comes first.\n- `fitness --policy=external --checks=<name,name>`: Select the checks a command line names: with --policy=external, a run runs exactly the checks --checks lists.\n\n## Commands\n\n- `fitness llms`: Prints a page that teaches an AI the whole CLI.\n- `fitness list [--json]`: Names every check with its summary, scope, stage, and whether this repo enables it.\n- `fitness check-versions`: Prints each check the repo installs with its version pin, as checkVersions in .fitnessrc.json sets them.\n- `fitness explain <check> [--json]`: Prints why a check exists, what it judges, and the config keys it reads.\n- `fitness docs [topic]`: Lists the guide topics, or prints one, from the code's doc comments.\n- `fitness check --list` or `fitness check <name> [args]`: Prints every built-in check name, one per line, or runs one check by name, a repo's own check included.\n- `fitness config [--json] [--policy=..] [--checks=..]` or `fitness config schema`: Shows the resolved settings and each check's state, or prints a JSON Schema for the config file.\n- `fitness init`: Installs the shared git hooks into this repo.\n- `fitness hook <name>`: Runs a git hook: commit-msg, pre-commit, or pre-push.\n- `fitness boxes <report.json> <page.html>`: Draws a --json report as an HTML box diagram.\n- `fitness perf <report.json>`: Prints each check's duration from a --json report, slowest first.\n- `fitness strip-front-matter`: Deletes the fitness keys from tracked Markdown files' front matter, and any block they leave empty.\n- `fitness adr-migrate [--dry-run] [--repo=owner/name] [--branch=main]`: Moves each ADR file into an `ADR` issue and points its links there.\n- `fitness comments [path...]`: Prints each code comment block of the tracked files, or of those under the named paths, with its line, language and kind.\n- `fitness branch-check`: Judges this branch's PR and the issues it closes locally, posting nothing.\n- `fitness pr-check`: Judges pull request titles and descriptions, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness dependabot-pr`: Completes a Dependabot pull request so pr-check judges it like a person's, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness issue-check`: Judges an issue by its kind label and comments the result, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness plan-check`: Judges Plan issues only, as issue-check does, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness close-check`: Reopens an issue closed with unchecked items, or closes the parent it finishes, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness dupe-check`: Notes when a new issue resembles an older one, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness comment-check --blob <text>` or `fitness comment-check`: Judges a drafted comment, where - or no text reads stdin, or judges issue and PR comments and reply, in GitHub Actions.\n- `fitness claude-hook`: Judges what a Claude Code hook event on stdin names.\n\n## Checks\n\n`fitness explain <check>` prints a check's whole documentation.\n\n- `local-branch`: Runs PR and Plan judgments locally.\n\n## Topics\n\n- `fitness docs config`: Package conf loads .fitnessrc.json — the Go runner's configuration file.\n- `fitness docs coverage`: Package goal holds the coverage goal every coverage check judges by, whichever language it measures: every counted line, branch or statement covered.\n- `fitness docs github`: cspell:ignore approv aproved dont doesnt didnt cant wont isnt wasnt arent\n- `fitness docs hooks`: Package hooks embeds the canonical may-journal git hooks that `fitness init` installs into a consumer repo.\n- `fitness docs languages`: Package lang defines what a language module brings to a run and orders what modules claim against what is installed or embedded.\n- `fitness docs local-checks`: Package repocheck finds a repo's own checks (ADR 0009, ADR 0012) and builds the Go ones, as docs/local-checks.md describes.\n- `fitness docs protocol`: Package checkkit is the contract between the fitness runner and a check binary, which prints one JSON Result on stdout.\n"
